lesson: 191
title: The comparison model: times as many, and the total
level: primary-3
stars: 0
explain: Draw the smaller amount as one bar, a unit. «Three times as many» is three such units side by side.
explain: Together the two amounts are 1 + 3 = 4 units, so one unit is the total divided by 4.
explain: Tom has 3 times as many stamps as Ann, and together they have 48: 4 units = 48, 1 unit = 12, so Tom has 3 × 12 = 36.
vars: a=name b=name t=thing x=3..30 k=2..6
let: y = k * x
let: s = x + y
need: s <= 200
answer: y
exercise: {a} has {k} times as many {t} as {b}. Together they have {s} {t}. How many {t} does {a} have?
exercise: {a} and {b} have {s} {t} altogether. {a} has {k} times as many as {b}. How many {t} does {a} have?
exercise: A farm has {s} sheep and cows. There are {k} times as many sheep as cows. How many sheep are there?
exercise: There are {s} children in the club. There are {k} times as many girls as boys. How many girls are in the club?
exercise: {a} and {b} share {s} {t} so that {a} gets {k} times as many as {b}. How many {t} does {a} get?
exercise: A ribbon {s} cm long is cut into two pieces. The long piece is {k} times as long as the short piece. How long is the long piece in centimetres?
test: For every one of {b}'s {t}, {a} has {k}. Between them they have {s}. How many {t} belong to {a}?
test: {s} {t} lie in two boxes, a red one and a blue one. The red box holds {k} times as many as the blue box. How many {t} are in the red box?
test: How many {t} are in {a}'s collection, if it is {k} times the size of {b}'s and the two collections make {s} {t}?
test: Last spring a school planted {s} trees, all oaks or pines. The number of oaks was {k} times the number of pines. How many oaks were planted?
exercise: {a} and {b} have {s} {t} in total. {a} has {k} times as many {t} as {b}. How many {t} belong to {a}?
exercise: How many {t} does {a} have if {a.he} has {k} times as many as {b} and they have {s} {t} combined?
exercise: A school in Vermont has {s} pupils who walk or cycle to school. {k} times as many walk as cycle. How many pupils walk?
exercise: {a} has {k} times as many {t} as {b}; together they have {s}. How many does {a} have?
exercise: On a pond in Canada there are {s} ducks and geese. There are {k} times as many ducks as geese. How many ducks are there?
exercise: {a} and {b} in Leeds collect {t}. {a}'s pile is {k} times as big as {b}'s, and the two piles have {s} {t} in all. How many {t} are in {a}'s pile?
exercise: A jar holds {s} beads, red and green. There are {k} times as many red beads as green ones. How many red beads are there?
exercise: {s} people came to a concert in Dublin, adults and children. The number of adults was {k} times the number of children. How many adults came?
test: {a} owns {k} times the number of {t} that {b} owns. Between them they own {s}. How many {t} does {a} own?
test: On a farm in Iowa there are {s} pigs and goats, and the pigs number {k} times as many as the goats. Find how many pigs live on the farm.
test: How many {t} does {a} keep, if {a.he} and {b} split {s} {t} so that {a.his} share is {k} times {b}'s?
test: A team in Perth scored {s} points in two halves, with {k} times as many points in the second half as in the first. How many points did it score in the second half?
test: {b} has some {t}. {a} has {k} times that many. The two of them have {s} {t} altogether. How many {t} does {a} have?
test: A bookshop in Bristol sold {s} books in a week, paperbacks and hardbacks. It sold {k} times as many paperbacks as hardbacks. How many paperbacks were sold?
